Subject: [#94-10-618] Anti-Statism not Consistent with Boundless Expansion

Aside from the Austrian idea of the problems of centralized calculation,
there's also the snafu principle. In a hierarchy, people are very unlikely
to tell the truth to those who can punish them. As a result, the people
at the top are even less likely to know what's going on than Von Mises
suggests--hierarchs aren't just ignorant, they're being lied to.

The snafu principle is from Wilson and Shea's _Illuminatus!_--a very
strange parody of conspiracy theories.

Nancy Lebovitz

